---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { site } from '../../site';

// The code shown here is the samples' own source, read at build time, so the page and the tested
// files never drift apart.
import honoAddon from '../../../../../services/template/src/addon.ts?raw';
import honoIndex from '../../../../../services/template/src/index.ts?raw';
import nodeAddon from '../../../../../examples/node-addon/addon.mjs?raw';
import nodeServer from '../../../../../examples/node-addon/server.mjs?raw';
import pythonAddon from '../../../../../examples/python-addon/addon.py?raw';
import seasonalAddon from '../../../../../examples/seasonal-catalogs/src/addon.ts?raw';
import seasonalData from '../../../../../examples/seasonal-catalogs/src/catalogs.json';

const tree = (path: string) => `${site.repoUrl}/tree/main/${path}`;

// Each season's first book, then "…" where the rest would be.
const seasonalExcerpt = JSON.stringify(
  {
    catalogs: seasonalData.catalogs.map(({ id, name, metas }) => ({
      id,
      name,
      metas: [metas[0], 'MORE'],
    })),
  },
  null,
  2,
).replaceAll('"MORE"', '…');

const seasons = seasonalData.catalogs.map(({ name, metas }) => ({
  name,
  books: metas.map((m) => `${m.name} (${m.author})`),
}));

const honoRun = `
cd services/template
pnpm dev             # http://localhost:8787/manifest.json
pnpm test
`;

const nodeRun = `
cd examples/node-addon
npm start            # http://localhost:8787/manifest.json
npm test             # node:test, no dependencies
`;

const pythonRun = `
cd examples/python-addon
python3 addon.py     # http://localhost:8787/manifest.json
python3 -m unittest
`;

const seasonalRun = `
cd examples/seasonal-catalogs
pnpm dev             # http://localhost:8787/manifest.json
pnpm test
`;

// The samples link the SDK from the monorepo; a copy outside it installs the SDK from npm.
const fromNpm = `
npm pkg delete dependencies.@lelap/sdk
npm install @lelap/sdk
`;

const toc: [string, string][] = [
  ['hono', 'Hono on Workers'],
  ['node', 'Plain Node'],
  ['python', 'Python'],
  ['seasonal', 'Hard-coded catalogs'],
  ['checks', 'What every sample checks'],
];
---

<Docs
  title="Samples"
  description={`Small, tested ${site.name} add-ons in TypeScript with Hono, plain Node and Python, plus one with hard-coded catalogs.`}
  toc={toc}
>
  <h1>Samples</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Five small add-ons, each runnable and tested in the repository. The first three serve the same
    two public-domain LibriVox recordings with one catalog, meta and streams; pick the one closest
    to your stack and replace the data with your own source. The last answers other add-ons’ book
    pages from its files, and has <a href="/docs/mapping-files/">a page of its own</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Sample</th>
          <th scope="col">Needs</th>
          <th scope="col">Source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <a href="#hono">Hono on Workers</a>
          </td>
          <td>
            Node 20+, <code>@lelap/sdk</code>, Hono, Wrangler
          </td>
          <td>
            <a href={tree('services/template')}>
              <code>services/template</code>
            </a>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <a href="#node">Plain Node</a>
          </td>
          <td>Node 20+, nothing to install</td>
          <td>
            <a href={tree('examples/node-addon')}>
              <code>examples/node-addon</code>
            </a>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <a href="#python">Python</a>
          </td>
          <td>Python 3.9+, nothing to install</td>
          <td>
            <a href={tree('examples/python-addon')}>
              <code>examples/python-addon</code>
            </a>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <a href="#seasonal">Hard-coded catalogs</a>
          </td>
          <td>
            Node 20+, <code>@lelap/sdk</code>, Wrangler
          </td>
          <td>
            <a href={tree('examples/seasonal-catalogs')}>
              <code>examples/seasonal-catalogs</code>
            </a>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <a href="/docs/mapping-files/#sample">Source for book pages</a>
          </td>
          <td>
            Node 20+, <code>@lelap/sdk</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            <a href={tree('examples/source-addon')}>
              <code>examples/source-addon</code>
            </a>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="hono">Hono on Workers</h2>
  <p>
    The template: <code>@lelap/sdk</code> does the routing, CORS, caching headers and checks every
    answer against the protocol, so the code is the manifest and a handler per resource, plus a
    settings page (<code>configure</code>) and <code>health</code>. It runs on Cloudflare Workers,
    and the same <code>addon.fetch</code> runs on Deno, Bun and Node. The{' '}
    <a href="/docs/sdk/">SDK guide</a> explains each piece; <code>src/books.ts</code> holds the
    data.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={honoAddon} label="services/template/src/addon.ts" />
  <CodeBlock code={honoIndex} label="services/template/src/index.ts" />
  <CodeBlock code={honoRun} label="Terminal" />
  <p>
    In the repository the template uses the SDK from the workspace. Copy the folder out to start
    your own add-on, then install the SDK from npm:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={fromNpm} label="Terminal" />

  <h2 id="node">Plain Node</h2>
  <p>
    The protocol by hand with <code>node:http</code>: no framework, no SDK, no dependencies.{' '}
    <code>route()</code> turns a path into a status and a JSON body, and the server adds the
    headers. Read it to see everything an add-on has to get right; it is also the shape to follow in
    any other language. It answers <code>/health</code> too; for a settings page without the SDK,
    follow the <a href="/docs/protocol/#configure">configure flow</a>.
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>
      <code>examples/node-addon/addon.mjs</code>
    </summary>
    <CodeBlock code={nodeAddon} />
  </details>
  <CodeBlock code={nodeServer} label="examples/node-addon/server.mjs" />
  <CodeBlock code={nodeRun} label="Terminal" />

  <h2 id="python">Python</h2>
  <p>
    The same add-on with Python’s standard library: <code>http.server</code> for serving and{' '}
    <code>urllib.parse</code> for IDs and extras. Nothing to install. For more than a handful of
    listeners, keep <code>route()</code> and serve it from the WSGI or ASGI framework you already
    use.
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>
      <code>examples/python-addon/addon.py</code>
    </summary>
    <CodeBlock code={pythonAddon} />
  </details>
  <CodeBlock code={pythonRun} label="Terminal" />

  <h2 id="seasonal">Hard-coded catalogs</h2>
  <p>
    A catalog doesn’t have to come from a database or an API. This add-on serves two fixed lists
    from a JSON file, with no code beyond the SDK:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {seasons.map((season) => (
      <li>
        <strong>{season.name}</strong>: {season.books.join(', ')}.
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>
    It only answers <code>catalog</code>. Every book has a <code>librivox:</code> ID, the
    archive.org identifier of a LibriVox recording, so the LibriVox add-on that {site.name} ships
    with answers its meta and streams. Listeners get two new rows on Home, and the audio comes
    straight from archive.org.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={seasonalExcerpt}
    label="examples/seasonal-catalogs/src/catalogs.json (excerpt)"
  />
  <CodeBlock code={seasonalAddon} label="examples/seasonal-catalogs/src/addon.ts" />
  <CodeBlock code={seasonalRun} label="Terminal" />
  <p>
    A copy outside the repository installs the SDK from npm the same way as the{' '}
    <a href="#hono">template</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    To change the lists, edit the JSON: each catalog has an <code>id</code>, a <code>name</code> and
    a list of <a href="/docs/protocol/#meta-preview">MetaPreview</a> objects. The add-on checks the
    file against the protocol when it starts. A recording’s ID is the last part of its archive.org
    address: <code>archive.org/details/christmascarol_librivox</code> is{' '}
    <code>librivox:christmascarol_librivox</code>. <code>ol:</code> IDs of Open Library works work
    too; the LibriVox add-on looks up recordings for them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Catalogs of your own recordings need <code>meta</code> and <code>stream</code> as well: start
    from the template instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checks">What every sample checks</h2>
  <p>Each sample’s tests run in CI with the rest of the repository. Between them they check:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the manifest, a catalog with <code>skip</code> paging, meta and both kinds of stream;
    </li>
    <li>
      percent-encoded IDs, and <code>404</code> JSON errors for unknown routes and IDs;
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code> on every answer and <code>204</code> for{' '}
      <code>OPTIONS</code>;
    </li>
    <li>that every answer passes the protocol’s schemas, Python’s included.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Before you share your own add-on, paste its manifest into the{' '}
    <a href="/docs/manifest-builder/#check">manifest checker</a>, then add it in the app.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  details {
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  summary {
    padding: 12px 20px;
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--fg-soft);
  }

  details[open] summary {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }

  details :global(.code) {
    border: none;
    border-radius: 0 0 14px 14px;
  }
</style>
